{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>This day I begin to put your dread.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Ta'anit 20a) — from a gezerah shavah of 'I begin' / 'I begin' — that Moshe stopped the sun, in the same way that Yehoshua stopped it. And he hinted at this in the word 'I begin,' for this was indeed the beginning that leads to the completion of the matter, since the completion could not come about without this beginning, because the nations trusted in the power of the constellations and most of them worshipped the sun. And so the spies said that they have great and fortified cities in the heavens (Deuteronomy 1:28). And they did not say 'in the heavens' by way of exaggeration, but they meant that all the source of their strength and might was from the side of the constellations in the heavens; and when the Holy One, blessed be He, brings down a nation, the beginning of their fall is through His first bringing down its deity, and afterward it descends upon the kings of the earth. Therefore the stopping of the sun by Moshe and Yehoshua was the beginning of their fall, for when the constellation stands still from its motion, all who trust in it necessarily fall along with it, and they tremble and quake before it. Therefore the stopping of the sun was hinted at in 'I begin' / 'I begin,' to teach that their fall depended on this beginning. And concerning this beginning he said: 'See, I have begun to deliver before you Sihon and his land — begin to possess' (Deuteronomy 2:31), 'and Sihon came out' etc. Why did he say 'I have begun,' when He had not yet given anything into his hand? And if the gift of HaShem and His promise are as though already given, if so why did he say 'I have begun' — the completion would already have been too. Rather, it is certainly about the stopping of the sun that he said this. And it may be that this is also the opinion of Rashi, who said: the prince of the Amorites was subdued above, etc. — but the prince is not stated explicitly in Scripture. This is fine: the stopping of the sun is hinted at through the gezerah shavah of 'I begin' / 'I begin.'" "And the fact that Moshe sent to Sihon words of peace, even though HaShem had already told him, 'Begin to possess, and contend with him in battle' (Deuteronomy 2:24) — this is because Moshe knew through the holy spirit that was upon him that Sihon would not listen, and in any case he opened with peace, for the sake of the desecration of HaShem's name — so that the nations should not say that Israel comes upon them by stealth and indirection. And he learned this from Midbar Kedemot, according to Rashi's interpretation; for at the giving of the Torah as well, it was revealed before Him, blessed be He, that they would not want to receive it, and likewise it was revealed before Him, blessed be He, that Pharaoh would not listen." "And He said, <b>so that I may give it into your hand this day.</b> That is to say: just like this daily sphere, that is to say, the sun — it is given into your hand; so too will it be given into your hand. And from here is a correct allusion that Moshe stopped the sun."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {0 "The Talmud citation תענית כ is resolved as Ta'anit 20a based on the standard pagination; the identical teaching appears in Ta'anit 20a.", 2 "The phrase 'this day' is read as 'the daily sphere' (הגלגל היומי), i.e., the sun, based on the homiletic identification of יום with the sun's daily circuit."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:11:24.883747919Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}